  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 321 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather continues Friday and Saturday ahead of the next \ncold front.  Daytime highs will rise another couple of degrees and \ntop out in the upper 60s to low 70s both days.  Guidance is showing \na mostly clear sky and lighter winds on Thursday night/Friday \nmorning which will produce better radiational cooling than tonight \nand Wednesday night.  Lows Friday morning therefore drop into the \nlow to mid 40s once again despite the warmer start to the night. \nHowever, the ridge which has been blocking moisture return from the \nGulf will be weakening and low level moisture is forecast to flow \nback into the Mid Mississippi Valley ahead of the next short wave. \nThe GFS even prints out some light QPF over eastern Missouri and \nwestern Illinois due to the increasing low level moisture \nconvergence.  This is fairly typical, and does not result in \nmeasurable precipitation in most cases. However, forecast soundings \ndo indicate increasing low level clouds which will limit radiational \ncooling and keep temperatures from falling below 45 to 50 degrees.\n\nGlobal guidance continues to show a relatively strong short wave \nmoving off the Rockies into the Great Plains on Saturday.  The cold \nfront accompanying this wave moves into northwest Missouri Saturday \nnight and through our forecast area Sunday.  This is slower than \nprevious runs, and both models are now printing out more QPF \nSaturday night and Sunday than they were on yesterday's runs.  NBM \nPoPs have jumped to 60-80 percent between 06-12Z Sunday across much \nof central and northeast Missouri into west central Illinois.  But \ndaytime PoPs on Sunday are only 30-40%.  This drop is significant, \nand looks discontinuous when compared with the previous period, and \nwith the higher QPF amounts and areal coverage of that precip in the \nglobal guidance.  The LREF is also showing higher probabilities for \nboth measurable rain, and rainfall of 0.10+ inches.  Therefore it \nlooks like the NBM's low to mid chance PoPs are too low ahead of the \nfront on Sunday.  Will not increase PoPs in the official forecast at \nthis juncture though since there's some evidence that the models are \nvarying on the speed of the front.  Another cool high pressure \nsystem builds into the Midwest behind the front Monday and Tuesday. \nCurrent indications are that temperatures will be similar to last \nweekend's cold snap with highs in the low to mid 40s and lows in the \n20s early next week.",
